{"name":"Electron Blue","hex":"#0881d1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The subatomic particle having a negative charge and orbiting the nucleus; the flow of electrons in a conductor constitutes electricity.","Alloys of magnesium and other metals, like aluminum or zinc, that were manufactured by the German company Chemische Fabrik Griesheim-Elektron."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electron"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electron Blue","blocks":[{"text":"\"Electron Blue\" is a song by American alternative rock band R.E.M. It was released as the third single from their thirteenth studio album Around the Sun on February 28, 2005. Written by group members Peter Buck, Mike Mills and Michael Stipe, the song features a synthesizer-heavy arrangement. Its lyrics describe an electric hallucinogenic drug made of light, a concept which was developed from a recurring dream of Stipe's. Upon release as a single, \"Electron Blue\" peaked at numbers 43 and 26 on the singles charts of Ireland and the United Kingdom respectively. Michael Stipe performed on the Around the Sun tour with a blue band painted across his eyes as a reference to the song, his personal favorite from Around the Sun."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/49/R.E.M._-_Electron_Blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electron_Blue","wikidata":"Q1419705"},{"title":"Electron","blocks":[{"text":"The electron is a subatomic particle whose electric charge is negative one elementary charge. It is an elementary particle contained in the matter that makes up the universe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Atomic-orbital-clouds_spd_m0.png/330px-Atomic-orbital-clouds_spd_m0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electron","wikidata":"Q2225"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg/500px-Cyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg/1280px-Cyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg/500px-Cyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A beam of electrons deflected by a magnetic field into a circle[29]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACyclotron_motion_wider_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/J.J_Thomson.jpg/500px-J.J_Thomson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/J.J_Thomson.jpg/500px-J.J_Thomson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"J. J. Thomson","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJ.J_Thomson.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Millikan.jpg/500px-Millikan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Millikan.jpg/1280px-Millikan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Millikan.jpg/500px-Millikan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Robert Millikan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMillikan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Orbital_s1.png/330px-Orbital_s1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Orbital_s1.png/330px-Orbital_s1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In quantum mechanics, the behavior of an electron in an atom is described by an orbital, which is a wavefunction rather than an orbit. In the figure, the shading indicates the relative probability to \"find\" the electron, having the energy corresponding to the given quantum numbers, at that point.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrbital_s1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Asymmetricwave2.png/500px-Asymmetricwave2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Asymmetricwave2.png/1280px-Asymmetricwave2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Asymmetricwave2.png/500px-Asymmetricwave2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Example of an antisymmetric wave function for a quantum state of two identical fermions in a one-dimensional box, with each horizontal axis corresponding to the position of one particle. If the particles swap position, the wave function inverts its sign.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAsymmetricwave2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Virtual_pairs_near_electron.png/500px-Virtual_pairs_near_electron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Virtual_pairs_near_electron.png/500px-Virtual_pairs_near_electron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A schematic depiction of virtual electron–positron pairs appearing at random near an electron (at lower left)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVirtual_pairs_near_electron.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Hydrogen_Density_Plots.png/500px-Hydrogen_Density_Plots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Hydrogen_Density_Plots.png/1280px-Hydrogen_Density_Plots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Hydrogen_Density_Plots.png/500px-Hydrogen_Density_Plots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Probability densities for the first few hydrogen atom orbitals, seen in cross-section. The energy level of a bound electron determines the orbital it occupies, and the color reflects the probability of finding the electron at a given position.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrogen_Density_Plots.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg/500px-Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg/1280px-Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg/500px-Lightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A lightning discharge consists primarily of a flow of electrons.[132] The electric potential needed for lightning can be generated by a triboelectric effect.[133][134]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALightning_over_Oradea_Romania_cropped.jpg"}],"sounds":{"electron":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/En-us-electron.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/95/En-us-electron.ogg/En-us-electron.ogg.mp3"}],"blue":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/En-uk-blue.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/8f/En-uk-blue.ogg/En-uk-blue.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/85/En-us-blue.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/85/En-us-blue.ogg/En-us-blue.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/EN-AU_ck1_blue.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/9c/EN-AU_ck1_blue.ogg/EN-AU_ck1_blue.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Hinge on blue dumpster","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Hinge_on_blue_dumpster.jpg/500px-Hinge_on_blue_dumpster.jpg","ratio":1.3370226895406752,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hinge_on_blue_dumpster.jpg","share":0.4675},{"title":"Blue Mosque Courtyard Dusk Wikimedia Commons","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Blue_Mosque_Courtyard_Dusk_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg/500px-Blue_Mosque_Courtyard_Dusk_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg","ratio":1.4224014336917563,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Mosque_Courtyard_Dusk_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg","share":0.10375},{"title":"Hazy blue hour in Grand Canyon","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Hazy_blue_hour_in_Grand_Canyon.JPG/500px-Hazy_blue_hour_in_Grand_Canyon.JPG","ratio":1.4430577223088923,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hazy_blue_hour_in_Grand_Canyon.JPG","share":0.059375},{"title":"HIV-infected T cell (6813384933)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/HIV-infected_T_cell_%286813384933%29.jpg/500px-HIV-infected_T_cell_%286813384933%29.jpg","ratio":1,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HIV-infected_T_cell_(6813384933).jpg","share":0.0525}]}}